<p>When people think about <strong>how Japanese Wagyu is cooked</strong>, they often picture a small steak seared on a hot plate. That is an excellent approach, but far from the only one. In Japan, beef appears in styles as different as <strong>yakiniku, teppanyaki, shabu-shabu and sukiyaki</strong>, each built around different cuts, thicknesses, heat and ways of eating.</p>
<p>None of these techniques belongs exclusively to Wagyu, and not every Wagyu piece has to be prepared this way. What makes them interesting is how differently they handle highly marbled beef. The same ingredient can feel roasted and direct over a grill, delicate in broth or deeply savoury in a sweet-salty preparation.</p>
<h2>Yakiniku: small bites of grilled meat</h2>
<p><strong>Yakiniku</strong> literally refers to grilled meat. In the familiar Japanese restaurant format, small pieces are cooked on a grill at the table, letting diners control timing and eat each piece almost immediately.</p>
<p>This format suits Wagyu particularly well because there is no need for one enormous steak. Small portions brown rapidly, the fat softens and each bite reaches the plate hot.</p>
<h2>Why thickness matters for yakiniku</h2>
<p>A relatively thin slice needs very little cooking. If it is too thick, the surface can burn before the centre becomes pleasant; if extremely thin, it can move from browned to dry in seconds.</p>
<p>The ideal thickness changes with cut and marbling. Very rich A5 works well in small pieces because it needs little volume to deliver intensity.</p>
<h2>The A5 yakiniku challenge: fat and flames</h2>
<p>Grilling creates the same issue described in our <a href="/en/wagyu-pan-griddle-grill-which-method/">pan, griddle and grill comparison</a>: dripping fat can trigger flare-ups. With A5 this can happen very quickly.</p>
<p>Watch constantly, move pieces and do not let flames engulf the beef for long. The goal is grill aroma and clean browning, not soot or bitterness.</p>
<h2>How yakiniku is seasoned</h2>
<p>Yakiniku may be served with salt, citrus, lemon, tare sauces or mixtures involving soy, fruit, garlic, sesame and other ingredients depending on style. With premium beef, begin with salt or a light sauce before trying stronger flavours.</p>
<p>Small individual bites make experimentation easy: one plain, one salted and one lightly dipped in sauce.</p>
<h2>Teppanyaki: control on a metal plate</h2>
<p><strong>Teppanyaki</strong> is cooking on a hot metal plate. In specialist restaurants, the chef can work directly in front of diners and control heat zones, ingredient order and the doneness of each piece precisely.</p>
<p>For Wagyu, the major advantage is uniform contact with a flat surface. Rendered fat can be managed, removed or used for other ingredients instead of falling into a flame.</p>
<h2>Why teppanyaki suits A5</h2>
<p>A5 responds beautifully to short, controlled cooking. A plate makes it easy to portion the beef, brown each face and serve immediately. Vegetables or garlic can also be cooked in a tiny amount of the Wagyu's own fat.</p>
<p>This technique prioritises precision over fire. Its relative neutrality makes the original beef aroma easy to appreciate.</p>
<h2>Teppanyaki does not mean theatrical tricks</h2>
<p>Outside Japan, teppanyaki is sometimes associated with restaurants where performance is as prominent as food. The technical core is much simpler: cooking on a heated metal surface.</p>
<p>At home, a good flat griddle reproduces much of that logic without any theatrical equipment.</p>
<h2>Shabu-shabu: thin slices in hot broth</h2>
<p><strong>Shabu-shabu</strong> takes a completely different direction. Instead of seeking a browned crust, very thin slices of meat are briefly moved through hot broth, often relatively mild, and then eaten with dipping sauces.</p>
<p>The name evokes the swishing movement through the liquid. Cooking is rapid precisely because the slices are so thin.</p>
<h2>What shabu-shabu does to Wagyu</h2>
<p>Hot liquid softens and renders some marbling without creating grill crust. The result can feel delicate and clean. Rather than concentrating flavour through browning, the method highlights texture and melting fat.</p>
<p>The broth also gains flavour and a little fat as the meal progresses, so the experience changes over time.</p>
<h2>Which cuts work for shabu-shabu?</h2>
<p>The key is the ability to slice the meat thinly and evenly. It does not always require the most expensive cut. Several muscles can work if tenderness and marbling are appropriate.</p>
<p>Extremely fatty A5 can be intense even in nearly translucent slices, showing why format matters as much as total weight.</p>
<h2>Shabu-shabu sauces</h2>
<p>Ponzu and sesame-based sauces are common partners. Ponzu provides acidity and salinity; sesame adds creaminess and roasted flavour. With highly marbled Wagyu, ponzu can be especially refreshing.</p>
<p>There is no need to drown the beef. A modest amount lets its own character remain clear.</p>
<h2>Sukiyaki: sweetness, soy and deeper seasoning</h2>
<p><strong>Sukiyaki</strong> also uses thinly sliced beef, but its flavour profile is very different. Sweet and salty components, commonly involving soy and sugar, cook with other ingredients in the same vessel.</p>
<p>Regional and household variations exist. Broadly, some approaches begin by searing meat and adding seasoning afterwards, while others begin with a prepared cooking liquid.</p>
<h2>Why Wagyu works in sukiyaki</h2>
<p>Wagyu richness stands up well to sweet-salty seasoning when the beef is sliced thinly. Fat integrates with sauce and other ingredients, creating an experience completely different from a minimalist salt-only tasting.</p>
<p>Here the aim is not to isolate Wagyu flavour but to integrate it into a complete dish. A stronger sauce is therefore not a mistake; it is part of the style.</p>
<h2>Is raw egg compulsory with sukiyaki?</h2>
<p>In Japan, dipping cooked sukiyaki ingredients in beaten raw egg is well known. It is not essential, and the safety of raw egg depends on local production systems, handling and public-health guidance.</p>
<p>When cooking outside Japan, follow local recommendations on raw eggs and choose safer alternatives where appropriate.</p>
<h2>Which method shows the purest beef flavour?</h2>
<p>If the objective is to analyse the meat with minimal interference, teppanyaki with salt is probably the most neutral of the four. Yakiniku adds grill aroma; shabu-shabu avoids browning and emphasises texture; sukiyaki deliberately introduces sweet-salty seasoning.</p>
<p>That does not make one better. They answer different culinary goals.</p>
<h2>Which handles extremely fatty A5 best?</h2>
<p>Teppanyaki provides excellent control over rendered fat. Shabu-shabu naturally moderates intensity through very thin portions. Yakiniku demands attention to flare-ups. Sukiyaki integrates the fat into a broader dish with sauce and vegetables.</p>
<p>If A5 is new to you, a flat teppan-style surface is one of the easiest techniques to master. The other styles then reveal different sides of the beef.</p>
<h2>Do these dishes require A5?</h2>
<p>No. This is an important point. Yakiniku, shabu-shabu and sukiyaki can be prepared with many beef cuts and quality levels. Even within Wagyu, moderate marbling may offer a better balance in dishes with many ingredients.</p>
<p>A5 creates one particular experience, but it does not automatically improve every recipe. Match the grade and cut to the method.</p>
<h2>How to choose between the four</h2>
<p>Choose <strong>yakiniku</strong> for grilling, interaction and browned bites. Choose <strong>teppanyaki</strong> for control and a relatively pure expression of the beef. Choose <strong>shabu-shabu</strong> for delicate texture and a broth-centred meal. Choose <strong>sukiyaki</strong> for a more integrated, deeply seasoned sweet-salty dish.</p>
<p>You can also split one purchase into a small comparative tasting and try two different techniques. Comparing methods teaches you more about Wagyu than following only one recipe.</p>
<h2>The conclusion: Wagyu is much more than steak</h2>
<p><strong>Yakiniku, teppanyaki, shabu-shabu and sukiyaki offer four different ways to manage marbling, heat and bite size.</strong> None is the only authentic way to eat Wagyu, and none always requires A5.</p>
<p>Understanding these techniques greatly expands your buying options: a thin slice can be every bit as interesting as a thick steak, and the best cut depends on what you intend to put on the table.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>What is yakiniku?</h3>
<p>It is a grilled-meat style commonly involving small pieces cooked on a grill at the table.</p>
<h3>What is the difference between shabu-shabu and sukiyaki?</h3>
<p>Shabu-shabu briefly cooks thin slices in broth and commonly uses dipping sauces; sukiyaki uses a sweeter, saltier cooking and seasoning style.</p>
<h3>Are teppanyaki and griddle cooking the same?</h3>
<p>They share the principle of cooking on a hot metal surface. Teppanyaki is a specific culinary style, while a good home griddle can reproduce much of the same cooking logic.</p>
<h3>Do all these dishes need A5 Wagyu?</h3>
<p>No. They work with different cuts and marbling levels. A5 is an option, not a requirement.</p>
